# vercel deploy does NOT respect .gitignore for what gets uploaded -- these
# exclusions are load-bearing, not cosmetic. Verified via `vercel deploy
# --dry --json`, which showed Clients/ (real client CVs, personal
# documents), extras/, .agents/, .claude/, and the private report
# generators would all otherwise be uploaded to Vercel.
#
# This file MUST live at the repo root, not inside intake/, even though
# the intake Vercel project's Root Directory is set to "intake" --
# .vercelignore is read relative to the repo root regardless of a
# project's configured Root Directory (unlike .gitignore, which is
# relative to wherever the file itself sits). Discovered the hard way: a
# copy of this file inside intake/ silently failed to exclude anything,
# including intake/.python-version, which then broke the Python runtime
# version resolution during a real deploy attempt. Patterns below are
# prefixed with intake/ accordingly.

# Client folders — never upload client data
intake/Clients/

# Business docs/audit findings/spike research — not needed at runtime
intake/extras/

# Private report generators (contain client data or internal audit findings)
intake/generate_security_pdf.py
intake/generate_feedback_pdf.py
intake/generate_selfhosting_pdf.py

# Local Claude Code settings and JS-SDK reference skills — irrelevant to
# this app's runtime, never meant to leave this machine
intake/.claude/
intake/.agents/
intake/skills-lock.json

# Local pyenv version pin (3.11.9) -- conflicts with Vercel's Python
# runtime, which defaults to 3.12 and expects requires-python==3.12.*.
intake/.python-version
